# Parse columns, likeness and rhymes — 2026-11-11
#
# A probe toward the owner's open question of what a second renderer needs from a frame of plain data, what each subtree is alike in, and the shapes a corpus writes more than once.
# The owner asked to focus on creative expansion, exchange architecture, expression collectibility, artistic and poetic affordances, mathematical expression, preparation for parallel computing and shaders, and value for hypertemplating, for nuanced forms of wonder and for describing intricate expansions of cultural attention. Two of those words, hypertemplating and cultural attention, appear nowhere else in this repo; the readings below offer meanings for them and decide none.
# The probe was first named a frame, after the question's words, then a plate when the owner asked for naming alignment (frame names the [ ] container, mark the # marks); the owner found plate a bad name or a missed abstraction, and it was the abstraction: both were new nouns for the parse tree in another layout. The owner chose no new noun, the same day and before this entry was committed. The owner also asked to consider parser distinctions, and the rhymes had found four.

#>journal_2026_11_11_columns_likeness_rhymes
#:cache #!observation
#:layer #!pragmatics

^["entry"]{
 date: "2026-11-11"
 kind: #observation
 base: "main@4f116eb6 with the parse-columns probe and the separator change uncommitted"
 method: "built spw.parse-columns/1 and likeness in seed's experimental folder and spw form --columns and --rhymes in the CLI; laid this repo's surfaces out against the fingerprint; read rhymes across the plan surfaces and the wonders in the trials file; kept what the rhymes showed the parser dropping"
 limits: "nothing ran in a browser, a worker or a shader; the hash a shader would run is SHA-256 as written, untested on a GPU; rhymes were read in this repo only; the machine's load average passed 120 during the runs, so every timing is loose"
}

^["columns"]{
 carries: "one row per node in preorder by source position, in typed columns (kind, glyph, parent, depth, subtree size, start, end, word, word span), the fixed kind table, a per-surface glyph table, the parse receipt, and bands: rows deepest first, one band per depth"
 words_and_glyphs: "a row's word is what it names (an identifier, a literal, a label, a particle's or annotation's name, prose) and its glyph is the structure it writes (an operator sigil, a connector, a separator, a particle's aim); the word column is the first 32 bits of the word's SHA-256, the unit slot-hash uses"
 abstraction: "the columns are the parse tree, not a new product: the fingerprint's kind counts are a histogram of the kind column (a test holds them equal), and geometry bytecode's operator counts could be read from the glyph column the same way; glyph is nest-path's word for a container's punctuation, and mark stays the name of a # mark"
 laws: "1,342 surfaces in this repo laid out to 974,325 rows with no child span outside its parent, no node reached twice, no unknown kind, and the same bytes on a second layout of every 25th surface; three receipts say prose fallback. Rows equal the fingerprint total and siblings sit in source order by construction (both walk the same children, and the columns sort them), so neither shows that nothing was dropped."
 word_laws: "over the plan surfaces, 22,412 identifier, particle and plain annotation words hash exactly from their spans, which holds the two-character sigil rule, and no word span leaves its node"
 rhyme_laws: "650 rhymes and refrains over the 289 plan surfaces. That every refrain shares one content and no rhyme does holds by construction, since holes read the same word column content hashes. The independent law is the rebuild: before the parser kept separators, 4,672 of 4,802 instances gave back their template from their own words, ignoring spacing and comments, with 109 parting at a trailing comma and 21 at a [ ] frame's separators; with separators kept, 4,785 of 4,787 do, and the 2 left write a label after a . where the first instance writes none, a hole its template cannot mark"
 bands: "inquiry-loop's wip.spw lays out to 850 rows in 16 bands, the widest 173 rows: a bottom-up pass over it is 16 dispatches"
 cost: "60 plan surfaces took 32 s to parse, 2.3 s to lay out and 4.3 s to reduce to shapes, under load: the columns add little to a parse, and parsing is nearly all of a rhyme read"
}

^["parser"]{
 # What reading the plans as rhymes showed the tree dropping or splitting, and what became of each.
 frame_separators: "kept: the tree held a [ ] frame's items and not what separated them, so #[a ; b], #[a, b] and #[a b] read alike although this repo writes ; inside sets on purpose (order: #[outcome ; cost_or_disclosure ; compatibility]); a frame now records its separators aligned to its gaps, as a sequence does"
 trailing: "kept: a separator after the last item ([a, b,] or { a, b, }) was consumed and forgotten; frames and sequences now keep it apart as trailing, since it joins nothing"
 attachment: "left to wonder_trial_attachment: 400 sibling rows overlap in the plan surfaces, every one a Scope under an Expression; in a ledger line such as .[name](hash): followed by a quoted subject (the case inspected), the (hash) scope written after the operation's [ ] frame attaches to the enclosing expression, since an Operation has no scope slot, while the binding's span runs over it"
 digit_led_hash: "left to wonder_trial_hash_format: a ledger hash that starts with a digit lexes as a number and a name (2010b917 as 2010 and b917), so one ledger line rhymes as two shapes, .[_](__): _ written 198 times and .[_](_): _ 177 times with letter-led hashes; any written hash form has to lex whole whatever its first character"
}

^["first_rhymes"]{
 # What the rhyme reader heard over the plan surfaces with --min 12, heaviest first: first over 288 surfaces before this entry's own plan and the separator change, then again through spw form --rhymes --max-files 400 over all 289 after both, with the same counts and the cache frame in 95 files, this plan's own among them.
 cache_frame: "the plan template's cache frame, nine holes, in 94 plan files: the corpus's heaviest rhyme is the template every plan starts from"
 commit_ledger: ".[_](__): _ written 198 times in 55 files: the landed-commit line, its paradigm a name, a subject and a hash"
 lattice_lists: "_: [#_, #_, #_] written 211 times and _: [#_, #_, #_, #_] 168 times: the tag lists the plan template seeds, and their first holes hold the template's own keys (primary, tensions, transfers)"
 refrain: "a 653-node cut vocabulary repeated whole in three seeds under the cut's references"
 wonders: "in the trials file, three cards share one 104-node shape and others pair off; the shape of a wonder trial is itself a rhyme, and the fields a card adds or leaves out are where it parts from its kin"
}

^["readings"]{
 # Offers, not decisions; the open ones are trial cards wonder_trial_columns and wonder_trial_rhyme.
 exchange: "The columns are a product a host can take without the parser, and a shape is a name two hosts can compare without sharing text. A shape is 32 bits, so it only nominates; two hosts confirm by exchanging the kind and glyph columns of the subtree, and the words stay home. The schema is versioned and the kind table ships inside, so a reader refuses columns it does not know."
 collectibility: "A rhyme is a collectible that names itself: its shape is its id, its template its face, the words at each hole its paradigm, and its places its provenance. A card for a form would carry the shape, the template, the count, the places and the hosts that wrote it; a refrain is the same card found twice."
 poetics: "Rhyme and refrain are poetic affordances read from structure rather than sound, and the same columns offer more: enjambment (a subtree whose span crosses a line break), caesura (a band where the width drops), stanza (adjacent siblings of one shape), and a seed that recolours every word at once (the slot-hash seed, now reachable per node through the word column)."
 mathematics: "Likeness is a ladder of quotients, rung by rung: content keeps the whole subtree, shape erases its words, the skeleton erases all but its containers, and the census erases order too, so forms alike at one rung are alike at every rung below it. Two subtrees of one shape differ only in labels, and the words are the fibre over each shape. reduceUp is a fold scheduled by depth; the second mounted consumer's gradient fold (juxtaposition sums, & takes the least, ^ the most) is another visit function over the same bands."
 parallel: "Bands are dispatches: a pass costs as many dispatches as the tree is deep, and the widest band says how many lanes it can fill. The columns map to storage buffers one to one; what a shader lacks is only the hash, and SHA-256 is 32-bit arithmetic a shader can run, slowly. The columns' laws (one row per node, subtrees as ranges, bands whose order does not matter) are what a GPU result would be checked against."
 hypertemplating: "A template's holes are often rhymes themselves (the cache frame's holes hold stamps and branch names shaped alike), so a template of templates is a next reading: report the rhymes inside holes instead of covering them, and a corpus reads as a small grammar of nested forms with a paradigm at each level. It is one meaning of the word, offered, not assumed."
 wonder: "spw form --rhymes --sigil ? hears shapes that are wonders, not blocks that merely hold one. Nuance in wonder could be read as distance between shapes: two wonders one field apart are siblings, two that share only ?[_]{ } are strangers, and a wonder whose shape no other repeats is a new kind of question."
 cultural_attention: "What a corpus writes again is what it attends to. The paradigm at a hole is a census of that attention (which words fill a slot, and how many differ), and stream stamps date the instances, so a template's spread across time and hosts can be drawn: when a form first appears, who repeats it, and when its paradigm widens."
 parser_as_listener: "Rhymes are also a parser check: when two writings a reader would call different rhyme as one shape, the tree has dropped what tells them apart, and when one form splits into two shapes, the lexer has read one thing two ways."
}

^["next"]{
 owner: "read wonder_trial_columns and wonder_trial_rhyme, and the evidence added to wonder_trial_attachment and wonder_trial_hash_format"
 derive: "read the fingerprint and geometry bytecode from the columns instead of their own walks, so one reader serves them"
 inner_rhymes: "report rhymes inside holes, if hypertemplating reads as templates of templates"
 hosts: "rhymes across the mounted consumers, read-only, so a host receipt can say which forms the hosts share; spw form refuses a path outside the workspace it runs in, so this waits on how to read another root (open in the plan)"
 shader: "a WGSL port of reduceUp checked against the CPU columns, once the trial chooses rows"
 parse_cost: "spw form --rhymes reads through a corpus memo of parses, so a second read is cheap"
}
